(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-benign-skin-lesions-vignettes"]={"title":"Benign Skin Lesions Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 52-year-old carpenter has thickened, poorly demarcated, painless skin across both palms. The skin lines run through the thickened area. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Callus","Hard corn","Verruca vulgaris","Keratoacanthoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — broad-area pressure, diffuse thickening, no central core, painless. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 41-year-old runner has a tender lesion in the web space between the fourth and fifth toes. It is whitish and boggy rather than hard. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Soft corn","Hard corn","Callus","Plantar wart"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — clavus mollum, softened by moisture trapped between the toes. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a firm, movable, round nodule on his upper back. There is a small central pore, and pressing it expresses cream-coloured pasty material with an unpleasant smell. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pilonidal cyst","Epidermoid cyst","Lipoma","Dermatofibroma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the central punctum and the odour of the keratin contents. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"An 84-year-old nursing home resident has a reddened area over her sacrum. The skin is intact and the erythema does not blanch when you press on it. Which stage of pressure injury is this?","choices":["Unstageable","Stage 1","Stage 2","Deep tissue pressure injury"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — non-blanchable erythema of intact skin. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 6-week-old former preterm girl has a bright red raised plaque on her scalp. Her mother says the area first looked pale, then developed fine red lines, and has grown noticeably in the last three weeks. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nevus simplex","Pyogenic granuloma","Infantile hemangioma","Nevus flammeus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the earliest sign is blanching of the involved skin, then fine telangiectasias, then a red or crimson macule, then rapid proliferation. The superficial type is the commonest. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has several smooth, firm, deep red papules under 5 mm on his trunk. They blanch with pressure and have accumulated over the last decade. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Spider angiomas","Pyogenic granulomas","Petechiae","Cherry angiomas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — acquired, increasing with age, once called senile angiomas. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A patient with neurofibromatosis type 1 attends for routine review. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Intralesional corticosteroid to any enlarging lesion","A cutaneous examination at every visit","Excision of every cutaneous neurofibroma as it appears","Annual magnetic resonance imaging of the whole body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — assess for new neurofibromas or progression of existing ones. Plexiform lesions may be locally invasive, so their extent needs determining. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 29-year-old asks how to stop her calluses coming back. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Well-fitting shoes and socks, and pads inside the shoe","Apply sunscreen daily and examine the skin periodically","Avoid ear piercing and other cosmetic procedures","Reposition every two hours and keep the skin clean and dry"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — remove the friction and the lesion stops re-forming. Tight shoes and high heels are named as causes, as is wearing shoes without socks. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 4-month-old has a rapidly enlarging periorbital hemangioma that is beginning to obstruct the visual axis, and is to be treated medically. Which is the first-line option?","choices":["A beta-blocker","Intralesional fluorouracil","Oral isotretinoin","Intralesional methotrexate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — oral propranolol or topical timolol, whose mechanism is not well understood. Corticosteroids are the other named first-line treatment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 35-year-old with a keloid asks about the silicone sheets a friend used. Which is the most appropriate description of how they are used?","choices":["Applied for twenty minutes twice daily for six weeks","Worn only at night for the first month after surgery","Applied once weekly in clinic under an occlusive dressing","Worn twelve to twenty-four hours a day for up to a year"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — theorised to raise scar temperature and increase collagenase activity. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 4-month-old has a rapidly enlarging periorbital hemangioma that is beginning to obstruct the visual axis. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer for surgical excision as the first-line option","Reassure and review in six months","Begin treatment, because functional involvement is an indication","Continue serial observation, since most lesions involute"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — cosmetic concern, functional involvement, deep ulceration and infection all indicate treatment. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman is to have a keratoacanthoma excised from her forearm. Which margin does the lecture specify?","choices":["Five millimetres","One millimetre","Ten millimetres","Two centimetres"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — with Mohs surgery for large, recurrent or cosmetically sensitive lesions. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A newborn has a pink irregular patch on the nape of the neck that blanches with pressure and becomes much more obvious when he cries. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Telangiectasia","Nevus simplex","Nevus flammeus","Infantile hemangioma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the stork bite, a more superficial variant of nevus flammeus. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has a soft, painless, rubbery subcutaneous nodule about 4 cm across on his back. It is mobile and there is no overlying pore. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Epidermoid cyst","Dermatofibroma","Abscess","Lipoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the most common soft tissue tumour, soft and rubbery, usually under five centimetres. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman admitted after a hip fracture has a heel wound with full-thickness skin loss and yellow adipose tissue clearly visible in the base. No tendon or bone can be seen. Which stage is this?","choices":["Unstageable","Stage 3","Stage 4","Stage 2"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — full thickness with fat visible, and nothing deeper exposed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Four patients each have a hard, hornlike keratotic projection. Which of them best matches the epidemiology the lecture gives for cutaneous horn?","choices":["A 14-year-old girl with lesions on the eyelids","A 3-month-old with a bright red lesion on the scalp","A 28-year-old pregnant woman with a bleeding finger lesion","A 62-year-old Caucasian with a lesion on the ear"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — Caucasians over fifty, on head, neck and upper extremities. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A patient's keloid has been treated with laser. Which addition does the lecture say gives the best result?","choices":["Combining the laser with over-the-counter keratolytics","Combining the laser with oral antibiotics","Combining the laser with beta-blockade","Combining the laser with intralesional steroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — laser works by shrinking collagen or inducing microvascular thrombosis. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 62-year-old attends about a new lesion on his sun-exposed forearm. Beyond addressing the lesion, which counselling does the lecture say to offer?","choices":["Sunscreen, sun avoidance at peak hours, and skin checks","Avoiding cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing","Repositioning every two hours and barrier creams","Well-fitting footwear and padding inside the shoe"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the general education point the deck attaches to any new lesion in a sun-exposed area. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has soft, yellow plaques on both medial upper eyelids. They are asymptomatic. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Syringoma","Milia","Sebaceous hyperplasia","Xanthelasma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — collections of lipid-laden macrophages, most commonly on the medial eyelids. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 16-year-old has a moist, bright red papule with an epithelial collarette on the finger pad of her left fourth digit that bleeds readily. She wants it removed and asks which option is least likely to come back. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Laser, which has the lowest recurrence and the best cosmetic result","Observation, since spontaneous resolution is guaranteed within weeks","Surgical excision, though it scars the most","Cryotherapy, which has the lowest recurrence and no scarring"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it has the lowest recurrence rate and also provides histopathologic analysis, at the cost of the highest rate of scarring. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with obesity has several soft, skin-coloured, pedunculated papules on a thin stalk in both axillae and on her neck. She wants them removed for cosmetic reasons. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Scissor excision, cryotherapy or electrodesiccation, without anesthesia","Elliptical excision with five-millimetre margins under local anesthesia","Intralesional corticosteroid injection at monthly intervals","Referral to a vascular anomalies specialist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — anesthesia is stated not to be necessary. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"After drainage, the patient asks how to stop the problem recurring. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Avoid ear piercing and other cosmetic procedures","Keep the area clean and free of debris, and consider shaving or laser hair therapy","Avoid sitting for more than two hours at a time and use a specialty cushion","Apply an over-the-counter salicylic acid preparation twice weekly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and seek care promptly if an abscess occurs again. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman develops several spider angiomas during pregnancy and asks whether she will need treatment. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["They will persist for life and require laser in every case","They indicate liver failure and require urgent investigation","They will fade if she avoids sun exposure","They usually resolve after delivery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — those caused by oral contraceptives resolve after stopping the pill. No treatment may be needed, and pulsed dye laser resolves most lesions that persist. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man had an appendicectomy five weeks ago. The scar is raised, firm and red but stops exactly at the edges of the original incision. He asks what will happen to it over the next year. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["It will grow rapidly for two months and then regress over six","It will remain unchanged and grow only in proportion to his body","It should stay stable and then flatten with time","It will keep enlarging for months to years and will not regress"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that natural history is the reason observation is reasonable. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 2-year-old boy has a flat, blanchable, pink-purple patch on one cheek that has been present since birth and stops sharply at the midline. His parents ask what it will do as he grows. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["It will stay exactly the same size and colour for life","It will grow with him, darken and thicken, and will not involute","It will proliferate for a few months and then slowly involute","It will fade within his first year of life"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the vasculature dilates over time and the lesion may become a raised plaque. Psychosocial disability from facial disfigurement can be overwhelming. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"You are not certain whether one of those lesions is a basal cell carcinoma. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start light electrocautery to all lesions and review","Dermoscopy, which can distinguish the two, with biopsy if concern remains","Excise all the lesions with clear margins","Reassure, since sebaceous hyperplasia never resembles a carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — patients often present worried about exactly this. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A patient prone to keloids has just had a lesion excised. Which post-operative advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Apply an over-the-counter keratolytic to thin the scar","Take warm baths daily to soften the tissue","Do not stretch the immature scar, avoid hot baths, keep it clean","Massage the scar vigorously twice daily and expose it to sunlight"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — stretching provokes scar inflammation and overgrowth, and hot baths can aggravate surgery-induced inflammation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has a firm brown nodule on her lower leg that appeared after an insect bite. Dermoscopy is performed. Which finding would support a diagnosis of dermatofibroma?","choices":["An epithelial collarette at the base of the lesion","A central keratin-filled crater within a smooth dome","A peripheral pigment network with a central white mass","A moth-eaten border with uniform pigment throughout"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the white centre corresponds to the fibrous core. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 22-year-old lorry driver has sudden pain and swelling over his sacrum. There is a warm, tender, erythematous swelling that is now tense and fluctuant. Which is the most appropriate immediate management?","choices":["Incision and drainage","Oral antibiotics alone, with review in one week","Referral to a surgeon for definitive excision now","Laser hair removal to the natal cleft"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — an acute abscess requires drainage. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"An 81-year-old patient has a stage 4 sacral wound with necrotic tissue and slough in the base. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start oral antibiotics and continue current dressings","Reposition two-hourly and take no further action","Refer for surgical debridement","Apply a silicone dressing and review in a month"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — necrotic tissue, eschar and slough promote infection and impede healing. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 36"}]};
